**Root cause:** In `pool.mjs`, `output.push(await fn(item))` appended each result as its promise settled, so the array ended up in completion order, not input order.

**Fix:** Preallocate the output array and assign each result to its own index:

```js
export async function mapInOrder(items, fn) {
  const output = new Array(items.length);
  await Promise.all(items.map((item, i) =>
    fn(item).then((value) => { output[i] = value; })
  ));
  return output;
}
```

All tasks still start immediately via `Promise.all` (concurrency preserved, satisfying the `peak > 1` assertion), but each result is written to the slot matching its input position — including `undefined` gaps if a task resolves to nothing. No serialization, no behavioral changes beyond ordering.

